From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 9 Jul 2026 21:33:34 -0700
Subject: [PATCH net-next v1 00/15] net: flow_dissector: opt-in byte-identical fast paths + tunnel inner descent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

__skb_flow_dissect() parses packet headers with a generic protocol-graph
parser. This series adds an opt-in, static-branch-gated, straight-line
fast path for the packet shapes that dominate real deployments. Each
shape has its own gate under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/, every gate is
disabled by default, and when a gate is off the added cost is one
not-taken branch per dissect.

The series is byte-identical by contract: for any packet, the fast path
either writes exactly the flow_keys bytes the slow path would have
written, or it returns false and the slow path runs. That contract is
enforced in-tree by a KUnit equivalence suite (patch 14).

Patch 1 stands slightly apart: it is a small win for the existing path --
today every dissect takes rcu_read_lock() and does two dependent
rcu_dereference() loads to look for a netns BPF flow dissector program
even when none is attached anywhere; a static key (mirroring
bpf_sk_lookup_enabled) skips all of it in the common no-program case.

Patches 2-7 are the byte-identical common shapes (eth+IPv4/IPv6 x
TCP/UDP, VLAN/QinQ, PPPoE, single MPLS, IP-in-IP, plain GRE). Patch 8
adds per-shape counters (/proc/net/flow_dissector_stats); patch 9 bounds
the fast-path tunnel recursion at MAX_FLOW_DISSECT_HDRS.

Why not just optimise the existing loop
========================================
The slow path is a generic graph walk by design: one loop, one
proto/nhoff state machine, re-entered per header, able to express every
protocol relationship the dissector knows. That generality is the cost --
per-header dispatch, state saves, and branchy key-writing that the
compiler cannot flatten because the shape is only known at runtime. A
per-shape straight-line function knows the whole shape at compile time,
so the loads, checks and key writes schedule as one block. The two
approaches are complementary: the loop stays the single source of
semantic truth for every shape, and a handful of high-volume shapes get
a verified shortcut.

Tunnel inner-flow descent (patches 10-13)
=========================================
For VXLAN / Geneve / GTP-U / FOU / GUE the dissector stops at the outer
UDP header today, so every hash-based consumer (RSS, RPS, ECMP, bonding,
tc-flower, aRFS) sees the *outer* tunnel identity, not the inner flow.
All inner flows between a pair of tunnel endpoints collapse onto a single
hash bucket. GTP-U is the sharpest case -- no outer source-port entropy
at all, and it is the mobile-core data path where millions of subscriber
flows ride one tunnel between two endpoints.

Patches 10-13 add opt-in inner descent, each behind its own default-off
gate (net.flow_dissector.{vxlan,geneve,gtpu,gue,fou}_inner). The descents
keep the series' byte-identical contract: the slow path grows the same
gated descent (__skb_flow_dissect_udp_encap()) as the fast path
(flow_dissect_fast_udp_inner()), so the fast and slow paths never
disagree. The only behaviour change is versus today's
non-descending default, and it happens only when an operator turns a gate
on -- a deliberate, per-tunnel-type choice, because it changes skb->hash
for that traffic. The KUnit suite covers the descents the same way it
covers the byte-identical shapes: with the descent gate pinned on it
dissects each tunnelled packet through the fast path and through the slow
path and memcmp()s the result (patch 14).

The descents validate the tunnel header before trusting it: VXLAN
requires a plain RFC 7348 header (I flag set, all other flag bits zero
-- GBP/GPE defer), Geneve requires version 0, no options and no OAM,
GTP-U requires a v1 G-PDU with no option flags. Callers passing
FLOW_DISSECTOR_F_STOP_{AT,BEFORE}_ENCAP (RPS/RFS, tunnel drivers) keep
the outer tuple even with a gate on. tc-flower tunnel matching
(FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_ENC_*) is unaffected -- those keys come from decap
tunnel metadata via skb_flow_dissect_tunnel_info(), not from this
descent.

FOU and GUE (patch 13) are Tom Herbert's UDP encapsulations. Unlike
VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U they have no well-known UDP port, so a packet cannot
be recognised from the wire alone; the dissector consults the per-netns
FOU table, reached from the built-in dissector through a small registered
ops hook (the fou module is tristate). GUE is self-describing; direct FOU
carries its inner protocol in that table rather than on the wire.

CPU for the descents is ~net-zero (the inner work cancels the outer
saving); the value is the inner-flow hash distribution/identity, and it
holds for IPv4 and IPv6 inner flows and underlays.

Performance summary
===================
Measured across 3 ISAs and 8 microarchitectures with byte-identical
verification per shape (userspace A/B harness compiling the dissector
both ways from the same source). Two instruments,
labelled below: "allshapes" compiles every fast path in (the realistic
icache footprint) and measures each shape in isolation; "isolated A/B"
compiles one shape's path against the unmodified dissector (the upper
bound for that shape).

Reduction in dissector CPU cost, allshapes microbench. Each row is the
range across the 7 measured microarchitectures, worst to best:

    shape     worst    best
    ------   ------   ------
    eth_ip    -4.7%   -31.6%
    vlan     -18.1%   -38.5%
    qinq     -16.1%   -39.8%
    pppoe     -8.6%   -28.0%
    mpls      -6.7%   -15.0%
    ipip     -36.8%   -47.0%   largest: slow path re-parses inner IP
    gre        (byte-identical descent family, tracks ipip)

The tunnel inner descents (patches 10-13) are ~net-zero CPU; their value
is the flow distribution above, not a cycle saving.

Isolated A/B, eth + IPv4 + TCP (same source compiled two ways),
nanoseconds per packet, lower is better:

    microarchitecture                      slow    fast   delta
    ------------------------------------  ------  ------  -----
    x86 Zen 2 (Threadripper PRO 3945WX)    12.4     6.6    -47%
    x86 Skylake (Core i9-10885H)           10.6     5.6    -47%
    x86 Haswell (Celeron 2955U)            35.9    18.0    -50%
    ARM Cortex-A53                        119.0    61.3    -48%
    ARM Cortex-A72                         42.4    19.1    -55%
    ARM Cortex-A76                         19.4     9.3    -52%
    RISC-V SpacemiT X60                    98.2    49.3    -50%

The isolated dissect costs 47-55% less per packet, consistent across
three ISAs and eight microarchitectures; the relative reduction is
largest on in-order cores, indicating the saving is fewer instructions
rather than a microarchitectural artifact. IPv6 uses the same gate and
is byte-identical (x86 -39%, RISC-V -25%).

flow_keys -> skb->hash is the canonical flow identity consumed by ~50
call sites: ECMP/multipath, RSS, RPS/RFS, bonding/LAG, tc-flower, aRFS.
Everything that spreads or classifies a flow derives from it, so the
per-dissect saving applies to all of them at once.

Relationship to the BPF flow dissector
======================================
The fast path is invoked strictly after the netns BPF flow dissector
hook in __skb_flow_dissect(): if a program is attached and returns any
verdict other than BPF_FLOW_DISSECTOR_CONTINUE, the function returns
before flow_dissect_fast() is reached. Attached BPF dissectors therefore
always take precedence, and a system running one sees bit-for-bit
unchanged behaviour from this series (patch 1 additionally makes its
program lookup cheaper for everyone else). Dissects fully handled by a
BPF program are deliberately not counted in
/proc/net/flow_dissector_stats (patch 8).

The gates are global (a static key patches code shared by every netns);
per-netns dissector policy already has a mechanism -- the netns BPF
flow dissector -- and it keeps full precedence here.

Maintenance burden: why two paths won't diverge
===============================================
The slow path remains the single source of semantic truth and the fast
path is verified against it, not vice versa. Three layers enforce that:

  1. The eligibility gate: only the two standard dissectors and a flag
     subset ever enter flow_dissect_fast(); custom tc-flower dissector
     instances are structurally unaffected.
  2. Every shape is a default-off static branch: a kernel with gates off
     executes the not-taken-branch slow path only.
  3. The tunnel descents share their header-validation classifiers
     between the fast and slow paths (flow_dissect_{vxlan,geneve,gtpu,
     gue,fou}_inner_ok()), so the descent subset is defined once, not
     mirrored by hand.
  4. The KUnit equivalence suite (patch 14) dissects a corpus of
     eligible shapes, tunnel descents, deliberate fast-path misses,
     truncations at every byte boundary, and skb-mode entry conditions
     through both paths and memcmp()s the resulting struct flow_keys
     (and their flow_hash_from_keys()). Future divergence is a failing
     test, not a silent behaviour change.

The test earns its place: during development it caught real divergences
the out-of-tree A/B harness had missed -- the MPLS fast path skipping the
slow path's out_good terminal writes, the 4in6 descent not reproducing
the outer-IPv6 residue, the VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U fast path failing to set
FLOW_DIS_ENCAPSULATION because the inner leaf zeroed it, a VXLAN
test builder double-byte-swapping the header flags, and -- caught by a
hardware boot smoke with counter positive-controls, then pinned by a
regression test -- the dispatcher rejecting the STOP_AT_FLOW_LABEL
flag that skb_get_hash() passes on every dissect, which had made the
fast path unreachable from RPS/RFS/fq/cake entirely. All fixed in
this posting.

Robustness and safety limits
============================
The fast path descends into IP-in-IP, GRE and (patches 10-13) UDP tunnels
by recursion. The slow path bounds header descents at
MAX_FLOW_DISSECT_HDRS; patch 9 gives the fast path the same bound, and
the UDP-tunnel descents route through a shared helper that carries it
too, so a crafted overlay-in-overlay chain falls back to the slow path at
the same depth rather than driving one C stack frame per ~20 bytes of
linear header. The KUnit suite covers the tunnel-bound boundary (a nested
VXLAN chain past the cap), the non-linear-skb fallback, the STOP-flag
contracts, a gates-off negative (the fast path must never run when
disabled, observed via the counters), and a seeded fuzzer as a general
guard.

A separate RFC thread proposes an adaptive auto-enable controller built
on the patch-8 counters (it manages only the byte-identical shape gates;
the descent gates are never auto-managed, since enabling them changes
hashing and must stay a deliberate operator action).

Patch 15 adds Documentation/networking/flow_dissector.rst -- there was no
general flow_dissector overview in the tree. It explains what the dissector
is and who consumes its output, then documents this series' fast paths: the
byte-identical contract, the tunnel descents, and the break-even model for
when a gate is worth enabling. The per-knob reference stays in
Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst; the new doc links to it.

On tooling: this series was developed with substantial LLM assistance
(Claude, Anthropic) -- identifying the optimisation opportunity,
drafting the patches, and building and running the test matrix. Every
patch carries an Assisted-by: trailer in the
Documentation/process/coding-assistants.rst format. All of it was
human-reviewed and human-measured: the performance numbers come from
months of runs across the hardware above, and the byte-identical
contract is enforced mechanically by the in-tree KUnit suite rather
than by trust in either the tool or the author.

This series grew out of work on Tom Herbert's XDP2 parser project:
porting the kernel flow dissector into XDP2's userspace benchmarking
harness, to compare parsers on equal footing, is what made the
per-shape cost of the generic loop visible -- this opt-in fast path is
the kernel-side result of that analysis.

Testing: 3 ISAs / 8 uarches (x86 Zen1/Zen2/Skylake/Haswell, ARM
Cortex-A53/A72/A76, RISC-V X60), in-tree KUnit
(patch 14: 61 tests, also run under KASAN+UBSAN; per-patch W=1, sparse
and smatch clean) and out-of-tree A/B. Supporting data
(flow-distribution study, per-shape/per-arch matrix, break-even
derivation) lives in the author's XDP2 fork:
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under perf-results/ .

